  "knowledgePattern": "Interchange the axes to differentiate a rational power, then return the tangent",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Find the point of contact",
      "body": "<p>Put <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> in the equation. If the square root makes this awkward, set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msqrt><mi>y</mi></msqrt></mrow></math> and solve for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> first.</p>",
      "recap": "A square-root substitution must retain a nonnegative new variable.",
      "pitfall": "A negative value of t cannot equal the square root of y.",
      "pause": "Find the coordinates of the point.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b3-l04",
          "reminder": "A square-root substitution must retain a nonnegative new variable."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Swap the coordinates",
      "body": "<p>Reflect the point and tangent in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, the reflected curve is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mi>u</mi></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>",
      "recap": "A square root can be written as a fractional power before differentiating.",
      "pitfall": "Swap the point coordinates as well as the variable names.",
      "pause": "Differentiate this curve and find its gradient at the reflected point.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l18",
          "reminder": "A square root can be written as a fractional power before differentiating."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Swap the tangent back",
      "body": "<p>Write the tangent line in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>. Replace <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, then rearrange it into the requested form.</p>",
      "recap": "A point and a gradient determine a line.",
      "pitfall": "The reflected tangent is not yet the tangent in the original coordinates.",
      "pause": "Write and rearrange the original tangent equation.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-extra-straight-lines",
          "reminder": "A point and a gradient determine a line."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p><strong>1.</strong> At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msqrt><mi>y</mi></msqrt><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> is admissible, giving the contact point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>2.</strong> Interchange the axes, writing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>. The reflected curve is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Differentiate with respect to u: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mi>u</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, which is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>3.</strong> The tangent in these coordinates is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Interchanging back gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Answer A.</p><p><strong>4.</strong> This uses only the rational-power rule on the reflected graph. As a check, the gradients of reflected nonvertical lines are reciprocal, and the final line passes through <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>"
}
